--- jeramy b smith <ultrapenguin@netscape.net> wrote:
>
> I agree with some of what Dan says although I would
> choose different words and
> a different focus.
>
> Development is not so much 'splintered' as it just
> has no 'center.' If you
> read the history of the ppc sources, you'll see that
> this is how it has always
> been. BUT, development is become more centralized
> out of mere evolution. For
> instance, here is a piece of 'voodoo' known only to
> a handful; the #mklinux
> channel on EFNet usually has 2 or more kernel
> developers on. Today I
> discovered that a kernel couldn't be built with
> having an asm-m68k include
> directory in your source tree. Instead of submitting
> a patch full of ifdefs to
> this mailing list, I just told Ben who was there and
> he added it to his tree.
> Eventually, this will wind up in Paul's.

It's nice that that kind of communication can happen,
but how many people know about it? I mean, it's
through such a non-standard channel. Users need to be
able to voice their opinion/needs as well, and a lot
of them may or may not know about #mklinux, which at
first glance, one could assume does not have much to
do with LinuxPPC, although there is a #LinuxPPC as
well.

The decentralization is an important issue however.
Dan and I are not just spouting our anxieties about
dealing with LinuxPPC, we are looking for ways to help
out. More specifically, I am totally willing to throw
this coming weekend into sitting down and cranking out
a new website for LinuxPPC.org, based somewhat on its
current setup, but with a database backend, and a php
frontend, and including information and links which
Dan has gathered in his searches for LinuxPPC related
sites. Whether or not the current webmaster of
LinuxPPC.org is willing to help out with this or not,
I am going to do it, if not to simply put actions
behind our words. So, I guess you can take that as you
will, but we really just want to make the user's
experience with Linux on PowerPC systems as
pleasureable as possible so that we can gather a
greater userbase, and hopefully encourage developers
to work with Linux on PowerPC systems as well.


> Sure, things are not as centralized as they could
> be. There is no
> linuxppc-kernel mailing list so that all new patches
> can be easily reviewed by
> peers and announcement for to-do lists can be
> posted. I'll set one up on my
> dedicated majordomo server but this would depend on
> whether the maintainers
> would go for it. In my opinion, to-do lists are the
> most needed thing. Many
> kernel development tasks such as cleaning up messy
> code and documentation
> could be handled by some of the wanna-be hackers
> (like me) while people like
> Ani, Ben, Paul, Cort, and Geert are writing drivers
> and merging new features.
> To-do lists would also give people who want to get
> in on the deeper
> development process an idea where they can start.

Personally, I think that kernel talk should just be
conducted on this list. I thought that was a large
piece of the reasoning behind this list.

> Linux/PPC just needs a bit of small, easy to
> implement, non-annoying, project
> management. The maintainers do an awesome job based
> on the amount of help/time
> available but a little bit of centralization of
> communication could increase
> the amount of volunteers doing meaningful work.

As I said, I am perfectly willing to help out with
this, however possible. PowerPC systems have some
kickass software and I'd much rather see people in the
future running Linux happily on G5's or the new
gigahertz processors IBM is working on.


